(469, 529, 'Can coloured or turbid samples be measured?', 'Strong colour, turbidity or suspended particles may reduce light transmission and affect the measurement. Sample preparation or an alternative method may be necessary.', 5, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(470, 529, 'What routine checks are recommended?', 'Perform zero checks with the appropriate blank and verify performance periodically using suitable reference materials or certified quartz control plates where applicable.', 6, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(471, 530, 'What is a colorimeter?', 'A colorimeter measures colour numerically using defined filters, illuminants and colour coordinates or scales.', 1, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(472, 530, 'Why is instrumental colour measurement useful?', 'It reduces dependence on subjective visual judgement and enables consistent comparison between samples, batches and specifications.', 2, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(473, 530, 'How should samples be prepared?', 'Use a consistent sample quantity, container, orientation and surface condition. Liquids should be homogeneous and free from bubbles unless the method states otherwise.', 3, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(474, 530, 'Can ambient light affect the measurement?', 'Yes, particularly when the sample compartment is not fully enclosed. Follow the specified measurement geometry and close covers or ports correctly.', 4, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(475, 530, 'What causes poor repeatability?', 'Inconsistent sample positioning, contamination, gloss variation, texture, sample thickness, stray light and inadequate calibration can reduce repeatability.', 5, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(476, 530, 'How is the instrument calibrated?', 'Calibration is normally performed with instrument-specific white, black or liquid standards. Use clean, undamaged standards and follow the approved calibration sequence.', 6, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(477, 536, 'What is a probe ultrasonicator?', 'A probe ultrasonicator transfers high-frequency mechanical energy directly into a liquid through a metal probe, producing intense cavitation.', 1, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(478, 536, 'What is cavitation?', 'Cavitation is the formation and collapse of microscopic bubbles in a liquid. The resulting local forces drive disruption, mixing and dispersion.', 2, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(479, 536, 'Why does the sample heat during sonication?', 'Part of the ultrasonic energy converts to heat. Use pulse mode, cooling baths or controlled processing intervals when temperature must be limited.', 3, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(480, 536, 'How deep should the probe be immersed?', 'The probe should be immersed sufficiently to transfer energy without touching the vessel. Follow the probe-specific recommendation and avoid operating the tip in air.', 4, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(481, 536, 'What causes foaming or aerosol generation?', 'Excessive amplitude, shallow immersion, unsuitable vessel geometry or gas entrainment can cause foaming. Reduce intensity and adjust the setup as needed.', 5, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(482, 536, 'How should the probe be inspected?', 'Check the tip for erosion, pitting, bending or contamination. A damaged probe can reduce performance and may contaminate the sample.', 6, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(483, 484, 'How does an ultrasonic cleaner work?', 'Ultrasonic transducers create cavitation bubbles in the cleaning liquid. Their collapse helps remove contamination from accessible and difficult-to-reach surfaces.', 1, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(484, 484, 'What cleaning liquid should be used?', 'Use a solution compatible with the item, contamination and tank material. Follow the chemical supplier instructions and do not use flammable solvents in equipment not designed for them.', 2, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(485, 484, 'Why should items not rest directly on the tank bottom?', 'Direct contact can reduce cleaning efficiency and may damage the tank or the item. Use the supplied basket or an appropriate holder.', 3, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(486, 484, 'What is degassing?', 'Degassing removes trapped air from fresh cleaning liquid. This improves cavitation and should be completed before critical cleaning.', 4, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(487, 484, 'Can all jewellery and stones be ultrasonically cleaned?', 'No. Some porous, treated, cracked or delicate stones and certain settings may be damaged. Confirm material compatibility before cleaning.', 5, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(488, 484, 'How often should the cleaning solution be replaced?', 'Replace it when contaminated, when cleaning performance decreases or according to the process procedure. A visibly clean solution may still be chemically exhausted.', 6, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
(489, 485, 'How does an ultrasonic cleaner work?', 'Ultrasonic transducers create cavitation bubbles in the cleaning liquid. Their collapse helps remove contamination from accessible and difficult-to-reach surfaces.', 1, '2026-09-21 06:17:26', '2026-09-21 06:17:26'),
